{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: the installed-release listing. Head 39d1a37151ac794801a00f4d120320df898b608a, branch request/application-release-listing-20261027. Main f9aca97f is the merge base. The delta is 10 paths, 1,590 insertions and 1 deletion. Review request 3a7726a3, checker promise 17c071d4, request 521f57a1, builder promise b1fb81ea, planner ruling 2dc765ac (on f7825ea7). Primary artifact 8a1dec04 (the PD-2 note), first of 13. gs inspect shows it resting on promise b1fb81ea, request 521f57a1 and ruling 2dc765ac.\n\nRead order: the note in full; 521f57a1 and 2dc765ac; the production delta (scope-index-applications.ts and scope-index-applications-command.ts in full, plus line.ts, scope-index-command.ts and the docs/deploy.md paragraph); the two test files and the fixture; the raw control records; the gate record; the manifest and the sealed directory. I did not run any suite again.\n\nRequest conditions (521f57a1):\n1. Same release as R11 admission. Admission compares fields.release of the application-create delivery with APPLICATION_TRUST. application-install.ts sends that field from job.values[\"release\"] (line 224). It accepts the created result only when definition, release, semantic and presentation all match the pending job (line 230), and then sets the job's scope ref and moves it to active (line 236). So the listing's row (the active installation's release value and scope ref) is the release that admission checks. The publisher comes from the release manifest (line 182). The host is taken from the parsed release URI. That parser accepts only the canonical lowercase host, with no port and no credentials, so URL.hostname equals the host grammar of a trust row. The three fields map one to one onto a trust row {release, publisher, host}.\n2. Coverage. Installations exist only as native Rules items. I enumerated platform:rules@1 to @39 at this head: @5 to @12 carry an installation item, and all eight are in the listing's set. Admission names nine cohorts; the publication cohort shares rules@10, so the two lists agree. A Rules pin with an installation item outside the set gives unsupported-definition, which makes the report incomplete, not empty. Descendants carry the root installation's release, so a row per installed root is enough to set trust. In enforce mode an unregistered scope is refused before it is reached (scope-index.ts:133), so the manifest is the reachable set as of its capture.\n3. Metadata only. Each row has exactly scope, installItem, release, publisher and host. The URI, the additions, the declarations, the genesis and every other slot are dropped; the test checks a private sentinel is absent. Saved JSON has only manifestPath, manifestDigest, applications and errors. Exception text never appears; the privacy transport control shows this.\n4. Unreadable scopes. Each unreadable scope gets a fixed reason: a read refusal, malformed-summary, identity-mismatch, incomplete-summary, scope-not-active, unsupported-definition, malformed-installation or installation-pending. Any thrown read gives unavailable. Any error makes the run exit 1 with status incomplete. A malformed active sibling voids the whole scope. A pending installation keeps its valid rows but marks the scope incomplete, as ruled in 2dc765ac item 3.\n5. No writes. The command validates the manifest locally before touching key custody, then sends one signed-only summary GET per listed scope, serially. It sends no session, retry, pagination, mutation or registration request. Redirects are refused, each response is bounded to 4 MiB and 30 s, and the output is bounded to 16 MiB, with no partial file. Publication is exclusive. The note and docs/deploy.md state that host maintenance can still run, as 2dc765ac item 1 requires. They also state the genesis-hash limit (item 4).\n6. Witnesses and controls. The recorded Native Counter capture gives the none and one projections, on real Rules@5 summaries at heads 1 and 10. Forbidden and malformed are scripted derivatives, and the note says so. I read the four paired controls raw. Each mutant typechecks, fails exactly one named test (1 failed, 5 skipped), and restores projector SHA-256 f07250f6, which equals the head's bytes:\n   - drop row: test at line 81 gets an empty list;\n   - reject empty Rules: line 90;\n   - hide unreadable: line 105;\n   - hide malformed: line 127.\n   The two transport controls (repeat GET and exception text) fail their call-count and fixed-error assertions. The first drop-row control did not compile; it is recorded as inconclusive.\n7. Documentation. docs/deploy.md has one paragraph under APPLICATION_TRUST. No live read in the gate and no builder deployment.\n\nValidation:\n- The ordinary gate at 1cd66fe7 (tree 174b2b48) passed: exit 0 from a clean status, with all four terminal evidence lanes complete and 0 contradictory records. The note gives 1,133.0 s wall and 2,335.6 s child CPU (one run).\n- After the gate only the note and the manifest change, so the documents-only rule applies.\n- The manifest verifies against the sealed directory: 142 files, 0 bad, nothing writable, SHA256SUMS all OK.\n- check-notes f9aca97f..39d1a371 reports 0 warnings, and git diff --check is clean.\n- check-doc-links on an exported tree of the head: 707 relative links checked, 0 broken.\n\nArchitecture: sound. One pure projector and one Node operator command beside the existing scope-index verbs. It adds no server path, setting, migration or deployment. It reuses the strict Read/Summary validators, the native catalog and the release-URI parser.\n\nSecurity: no blocking finding. Output is limited to the three trust fields and two references. The run fails closed on every unrecognised shape. Two points for the planner:\n1. Freshness. The listing is only as complete as its manifest. In enforce mode a room created after the manifest is captured is missing from the run, and its release would refuse after the deploy if it is untrusted. Capture the inventory just before each listing, and deploy from that report without delay.\n2. A complete report lists releases; it does not decide trust. Add a row only for a release you mean to keep. Any other installed release then refuses fresh acts after the deploy, which is the intended withdrawal.\n\nSimplification: acceptable. nativeItemsAgree re-derives count consistency that the server already enforces; that is reasonable for an operator check of a remote reply. The installation-pin set repeats the admission cohort table; BR-13's version tables could own both. Nit: one line of the docs/deploy.md paragraph runs past 79 characters (\"makes the report incomplete: ... before deploying. The\").\n\nVerdict: approved at exact head 39d1a371. The builder lands. The landing closes request 521f57a1. R14 child 00abd5aa and parent 0f0eeab7 stay open. No builder deployment; the planner runs the listing for dap-grow, dap-demo and dap-lending and files the results under 521f57a1 before any further R11 deploy.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:8a1dec04969725a66b111bd2d3a16efcde4f2ec0","binding":"assigned","head":"39d1a37151ac794801a00f4d120320df898b608a","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:17c071d4071449b35e716dea2fbeab0ce0dd4cf9\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b1fb81ead56fdfc39eef978678430ebadc2e8200\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:796f1a099879117e9c9859264ec14f41fbc06d8c","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}